<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tuva</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tannu Uriankhai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tuva is a republic of the Russian Federation in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, on the upper Yenisei, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. Inhabited mainly by Turkic-speaking Tuvans, it was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>'s Mongolian frontier until 1911, became a Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1914 and was the nominally independent Tuvan People's Republic from 1921. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> annexed it in 1944, and its people largely follow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> and shamanism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tuva.jpg" alt="Tuva" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mysid · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tuva.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its borders changed repeatedly: Turkestan was divided in 1924, the Kazakh and Kirghiz autonomous republics became separate Union republics in 1936, <b>Tuva</b> was annexed in 1944, the Kaliningrad region was added in 1946, and Crimea was transferred to Ukraine in 1954.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…recognised from the eighth and seventh centuries BCE in cultures such as the Tasmola culture of central Kazakhstan and the early Saka of Zhetysu (Semirechye), while the Aldy-Bel culture of <b>Tuva</b>, whose royal kurgan Arzhan 2 held thousands of gold objects, belongs to the same early Scythian horizon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Kalmyk regiments carried the banner of the war god Daichin Tengri through the Napoleonic Wars as far as Paris, and traditional Tengrism persists in Sakha, Buryatia and <b>Tuva</b> alongside other religions.</blockquote><a href="/religion/tengrism">Tengrism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>…of weapons, horse harness and art in the animal style lies far to the east, at Arzhan in <b>Tuva</b>, in southern Siberia: a great mound of about 800 BCE, excavated by Mikhail Gryaznov in the 1970s, where a chief was buried with his retinue and well over a…</blockquote><a href="/culture/scythians">Scythians · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>After years of civil war on the upper Yenisei, Tuvan leaders with Soviet support proclaimed the People's Republic of Tannu <b>Tuva</b> in August 1921, replacing the Russian protectorate of Uryankhay Krai.</blockquote><a href="/state/tuvan-peoples-republic">Tuvan People's Republic</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tuva</b>, called Uryankhay or Tannu Uriankhai, had been under the Qing Empire as part of its Mongolian frontier.</blockquote><a href="/state/uryankhay-krai">Uryankhay Krai</a></li></ul></section>
